<%# The visitor's detected location — PUBLIC, no sign-in required.

    Detection is IP-based and runs for every request (Studio::GeoDetection), so
    the badge can always say where the browser appears to be:

      · a subdivision flag + code for a home-country visitor ("WA")
      · a country flag emoji + region name for anyone else ("🇨🇦 Alberta")
      · a red "??" when the location cannot be resolved at all — which is not a
        cosmetic state: under the fail-closed rule an undetectable home-country
        visitor is treated as BLOCKED, and the badge has to say so rather than
        quietly render nothing

    Red also means blocked-by-policy, or a simulated location an operator has
    pinned from /admin/geo.

    THE BLOCKED LOOK IS AN ATTRIBUTE, not a class list, so anything that knows
    the policy changed can repaint the badge by flipping one attribute — which is
    exactly what /admin/geo's live preview does while an operator ticks regions,
    before anything is saved.

    Locals are optional. With none, the badge reads the request's own detection;
    passing them renders a specimen (the style guide, a view test) with no
    session at all. %>
<%
  subdivision = local_assigns.key?(:subdivision) ? local_assigns[:subdivision] : geo_state
  country     = local_assigns.key?(:country)     ? local_assigns[:country]     : geo_country
  blocked     = local_assigns.key?(:blocked)     ? local_assigns[:blocked]     : geo_blocked?
  simulated   = local_assigns.key?(:simulated)   ? local_assigns[:simulated]   : geo_override_active?

  # A home-country subdivision flag is ONLY correct for a home-country visitor.
  # The lookup matches a bare two-letter code, so a foreign region normalising to
  # "CA" would be shown the CALIFORNIA flag — wrong, and wrong in the way that
  # looks right. Outside the home country we render the country's emoji flag and
  # never consult the subdivision art at all.
  foreign       = geo_foreign?(country)
  country_flag  = foreign ? geo_country_flag_emoji(country) : nil
  flag_path     = foreign ? nil : geo_subdivision_flag_path(subdivision, country: country)
  label         = subdivision.presence || (foreign ? country : nil) || "??"
%>
<% unless @_studio_geo_badge_css %>
  <% @_studio_geo_badge_css = true %>
  <style>
    /* One rule owns the blocked look, so a repaint is one attribute. */
    .geo-badge[data-blocked="true"] {
      background-color: rgb(239 68 68 / 0.1);
      border-color: rgb(239 68 68 / 0.3);
      color: rgb(248 113 113);
    }
  </style>
<% end %>
<span class="geo-badge inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded-lg whitespace-nowrap
  <%= subdivision.present? ? 'bg-surface-alt text-secondary border border-subtle' : 'bg-surface-alt text-muted border border-subtle' %>"
  data-geo-badge
  data-country="<%= country %>"
  data-subdivision="<%= subdivision %>"
  data-blocked="<%= (simulated || blocked).to_s %>">
  <% if country_flag %>
    <span aria-label="<%= country %>" role="img" style="line-height: 1; flex-shrink: 0;"><%= country_flag %></span>
  <% elsif flag_path %>
    <img src="<%= flag_path %>" alt="<%= subdivision %>" class="rounded-sm" style="height: 12px; width: 16px; object-fit: cover; flex-shrink: 0;">
  <% end %>
  <%= label %>
</span>
